<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>com.tradingbotapp.ftmo</string>

    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <string>/bin/bash</string>
        <string>-lc</string>
        <string>/Users/kaloyanivanov/TradingBotApp/ftmo_runner.sh</string>
    </array>

    <!-- Install with:
             cp com.tradingbotapp.ftmo.plist ~/Library/LaunchAgents/
             launchctl load ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.tradingbotapp.ftmo.plist
         and remove it with `launchctl unload` on the same path.

         WAKES HOURLY AT :30, EVERY HOUR, EVERY DAY. This schedule is a
         deliberate SUPERSET and is NOT the trading window. The window —
         16:30 to 11:30 the next morning, every day except Sunday, Sofia time
         (owner decision 2026-08-06) — is enforced by
         `ftmo_runner.within_trading_window()`, and THAT is authoritative.

         Why the rule is not encoded here. The window wraps midnight and
         excludes one weekday, which in StartCalendarInterval means six
         Weekday values x twenty Hour values = 120 dict entries that no one
         will ever re-read correctly. It would also be expressed in whatever
         the host's local timezone happens to be, whereas the runner resolves
         `Europe/Sofia` explicitly through zoneinfo and therefore stays right
         across the EEST/EET switch and across any change to the machine's
         own timezone. `autotrade_runner.py` splits NYSE hours the same way
         and for the same reason: the plist only has to be a superset, the
         code decides.

         Out-of-window wakeups are free by construction — the runner checks
         the window before opening the audit log and long before importing
         torch, so a wakeup outside 16:30-11:30 costs one settings read.
         Roughly 20 of the 24 daily wakeups are in-window on Mon-Sat and none
         are on Sunday.

         The FTMO day boundary (00:00 Europe/Prague) falls at 01:00 Sofia,
         inside the window, so the 01:30 firing is the first of each new FTMO
         day and `advance_state()` rolls the day-start balance and trailing
         high-water mark there, off the previous day's settled closing
         balance.

         FTMO's 23:55-00:05 Moscow maintenance window is deliberately NOT
         scheduled around. The venue stays the authority: a MARKET_CLOSED
         rejection is journalled and the run ends without placing anything,
         which is the correct outcome rather than something to predict. -->
    <key>StartCalendarInterval</key>
    <array>
        <dict><key>Hour</key><integer>0</integer><key>Minute</key><integer>30</integer></dict>
        <dict><key>Hour</key><integer>1</integer><key>Minute</key><integer>30</integer></dict>
        <dict><key>Hour</key><integer>2</integer><key>Minute</key><integer>30</integer></dict>
        <dict><key>Hour</key><integer>3</integer><key>Minute</key><integer>30</integer></dict>
        <dict><key>Hour</key><integer>4</integer><key>Minute</key><integer>30</integer></dict>
        <dict><key>Hour</key><integer>5</integer><key>Minute</key><integer>30</integer></dict>
        <dict><key>Hour</key><integer>6</integer><key>Minute</key><integer>30</integer></dict>
        <dict><key>Hour</key><integer>7</integer><key>Minute</key><integer>30</integer></dict>
        <dict><key>Hour</key><integer>8</integer><key>Minute</key><integer>30</integer></dict>
        <dict><key>Hour</key><integer>9</integer><key>Minute</key><integer>30</integer></dict>
        <dict><key>Hour</key><integer>10</integer><key>Minute</key><integer>30</integer></dict>
        <dict><key>Hour</key><integer>11</integer><key>Minute</key><integer>30</integer></dict>
        <dict><key>Hour</key><integer>12</integer><key>Minute</key><integer>30</integer></dict>
